WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.496
Access control starts with account creation and administrative roles.

00:00:04.496 --> 00:00:07.623
A personal deployment may have one trusted user.

00:00:07.623 --> 00:00:19.612
A team deployment may need separate accounts, administrative permissions, and clear expectations for who can configure models, manage users, upload documents, or view shared resources.

00:00:19.612 --> 00:00:26.518
The more people who use the system, the more important it is to avoid shared accounts and informal access.

00:00:26.518 --> 00:00:30.884
Authentication considerations depend on how the service is exposed.

00:00:30.884 --> 00:00:37.855
A local-only deployment has a smaller attack surface than a service reachable across a LAN or the internet.

00:00:37.855 --> 00:00:41.309
LAN access can still be sensitive in shared networks.

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:52.581
Remote access introduces more risk and should be deliberately designed with authentication, TLS at a high level, reverse proxy configuration, logging, and update discipline.

00:00:52.581 --> 00:01:01.833
Reverse proxies can help centralize TLS, hostnames, routing, and access policy, but they can also create accidental exposure if misconfigured.

00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:11.085
Operators should know which interfaces Open WebUI binds to, which ports are reachable, and what network paths exist from users to the service.

00:01:11.085 --> 00:01:15.971
A private AI workspace should not become public infrastructure by accident.

00:01:15.971 --> 00:01:19.946
Least privilege applies to both users and connected services.

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.748
Not every user needs administrative access.

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:25.940
Not every workflow needs every model or endpoint.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.892
Applications and users should receive only the access needed for their role.

00:01:30.892 --> 00:01:40.209
Controlled exposure protects prompts, uploaded files, compute resources, endpoint credentials, and the operational integrity of the deployment.
